WEBVTT

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.596
Which grain deal lost margin? Did the
storage bill increase because the rate

00:00:05.631 --> 00:00:11.124
went up, or because the grain stayed
longer? And if a buyer pays late, can we

00:00:11.159 --> 00:00:13.197
still pay our suppliers on time?

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:19.033
In this walkthrough, I’ll start with the
portfolio, trace an alert to a single cost

00:00:19.068 --> 00:00:24.125
item and then stress-test the cash
position. Along the way, I’ll show how to

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.853
compare deals, ask the analyst a follow-up
question and download an English report.

00:00:29.268 --> 00:00:35.450
The sixty deals are fictional. So are the
prices, customers and payments. You can

00:00:35.485 --> 00:00:41.668
use the dashboard and download the data
without signing in. The AI analyst and

00:00:41.703 --> 00:00:44.333
email features require an issued login.

00:00:44.765 --> 00:00:50.456
Start with the snapshot. It tells you the
date up to which results and payments are

00:00:50.491 --> 00:00:55.467
known. Here, the eleventh of November is
the snapshot date. The revenue card shows

00:00:55.502 --> 00:01:00.120
roughly four point six five billion rubles
across forty-five completed deals in this

00:01:00.155 --> 00:01:03.699
selection. It is not one day’s sales, and
it does

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:07.278
not tell us how much cash has reached the
bank.

00:01:07.693 --> 00:01:12.875
The dashboard keeps completed and active
deals separate. For a completed deal,

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:18.961
compare the original plan with the actual
result. For an active deal, compare the

00:01:18.996 --> 00:01:22.439
plan with the estimate at completion, or
E-A-C.

00:01:22.474 --> 00:01:25.917
The estimate includes costs that are still
expected.

00:01:26.332 --> 00:01:31.430
Now restrict the portfolio to completed
deals bound for Türkiye that were entered

00:01:31.465 --> 00:01:36.169
from the first to the thirtieth of
September, inclusive. Five deals remain.

00:01:36.204 --> 00:01:41.697
The date filter uses the deal date.
Shipment charts group those same deals by

00:01:41.732 --> 00:01:46.831
loading month. A deal entered in September
can therefore appear under a different

00:01:46.866 --> 00:01:48.410
month on the chart.

00:01:53.825 --> 00:01:59.163
Clear those filters and switch the monthly
view from revenue to tonnes and then to

00:01:59.198 --> 00:02:03.819
margin. The three measures tell you
different things. Use the mix view to

00:02:03.854 --> 00:02:09.550
compare buyers or crops for one month, and
the trend view to follow a measure over

00:02:09.585 --> 00:02:12.774
time. The question-mark buttons explain
what each chart counts.

00:02:13.182 --> 00:02:17.666
Next, open Alerts. Margin deterioration
and a missed milestone are different

00:02:17.701 --> 00:02:23.830
problems. A deal can appear in both lists,
so check the financial effect and the

00:02:23.865 --> 00:02:26.706
timing separately. The first ten alerts
are

00:02:26.741 --> 00:02:29.993
shown up front; the rest can be expanded.

00:02:30.408 --> 00:02:37.072
Filter for the deal ending in zero zero
zero eight. It has a storage overrun. Open

00:02:37.107 --> 00:02:42.934
the deal and compare the planned and
forecast costs. The cost lines show the

00:02:42.969 --> 00:02:47.540
charged quantity, the tariff and the
currency. Here, billable storage increased

00:02:47.575 --> 00:02:52.564
from fifteen to twenty-six days. Before
blaming the tariff, check whether the

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:55.076
extra days account for the increase.

00:03:00.491 --> 00:03:04.720
The timeline separates slippage from an
open overdue milestone. The benchmark

00:03:04.755 --> 00:03:09.759
table shows which rates can be compared
and which reference records were used.

00:03:09.794 --> 00:03:14.022
These are demo comparables, not live
quotes. The exchange-rate check is

00:03:14.057 --> 00:03:19.061
separate: it shows what the official
reference rate would mean for the deal

00:03:19.096 --> 00:03:22.162
without changing the rate stored in the
calculation.

00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:27.477
Switch to the twelfth of November, the
recalculated snapshot. The storage warning

00:03:27.512 --> 00:03:32.413
for this deal disappears because the
forecast was revised. Other alerts can

00:03:32.448 --> 00:03:38.993
remain. This is a comparison of two saved
views of the same portfolio, not a manual

00:03:39.028 --> 00:03:40.639
dismissal of the alert.

00:03:41.057 --> 00:03:45.846
In Comparison, choose two completed deals.
Start with total margin, then look at

00:03:45.881 --> 00:03:51.784
margin per tonne and at what happened to
the schedule. A deal that earned more in

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:56.979
total did not necessarily earn more on
every tonne. If you compare a completed

00:03:57.014 --> 00:04:02.175
deal with an active one, read the labels
carefully. E-A-C is still a forecast.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.122
The same view compares groups: destination
countries, buyers, crops, loading months

00:04:11.157 --> 00:04:17.350
or deal managers. Pick one reporting basis
so the two groups are comparable. In Deal

00:04:17.385 --> 00:04:23.164
Manager Performance, narrow the view to a
deal month and switch to variance from

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:28.146
planned margin. The results describe the
portfolios assigned to the managers. They

00:04:28.181 --> 00:04:33.129
do not, on their own, tell you who is a
better employee.

00:04:33.544 --> 00:04:40.035
The mixed-crop snapshot contains wheat,
barley and maize. Use it to compare crops.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:43.047
It is a different demo portfolio,

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:46.562
not another revision of the wheat-only
transactions.

00:04:46.973 --> 00:04:52.837
A profitable deal can still leave you
short of cash. The buyer may owe you money

00:04:52.872 --> 00:04:57.630
on the same day your supplier expects
payment. In the overview, working capital

00:04:57.665 --> 00:05:02.792
in deals adds buyer receivables and costs
tied up in active deals, then subtracts

00:05:02.827 --> 00:05:06.479
unpaid supplier bills. This is not the
company’s balance sheet.

00:05:06.894 --> 00:05:12.055
Now open the credit facility. The
month-end balance tells you how much was

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:17.250
drawn on one date. The monthly peak tells
you the largest borrowing requirement

00:05:17.285 --> 00:05:22.446
during that month. At the snapshot,
compare the amount already drawn with the

00:05:22.481 --> 00:05:28.041
remaining facility. The annual rate is
weighted by how much was borrowed and for

00:05:28.076 --> 00:05:30.838
how long. Interest coverage is a different

00:05:30.873 --> 00:05:34.036
measure: margin before interest divided by
interest expense.

00:05:34.451 --> 00:05:39.525
In the payment calendar, start with the
base forecast. Then choose the scenario in

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.634
which buyers pay twenty-one days later,
and calculate it. Look for the first date

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:49.742
with uncovered payments, the worst amount
and how long the shortfall lasts. You can

00:05:49.777 --> 00:05:54.487
open the daily breakdown to find the
payment that falls in the gap.

00:05:54.902 --> 00:06:00.164
Now try the response scenario. It combines
the recovery of some overdue receivables,

00:06:00.199 --> 00:06:05.055
a supplier deferral and a draw on
available credit. The shortfall is

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:10.353
smaller, but it does not disappear. Look
at the amount still uncovered before

00:06:10.388 --> 00:06:12.390
deciding what to do next.

00:06:19.805 --> 00:06:25.112
These scenarios do not change the original
deal or make a payment. When you filter

00:06:25.147 --> 00:06:30.097
down to particular deals, you can see
their cash flows, but the company’s cash

00:06:30.132 --> 00:06:33.302
and undrawn limit cannot be allocated to
that selection.

00:06:33.723 --> 00:06:39.106
After signing in, open the analyst in the
deal card. I’ll ask: “Compare storage

00:06:39.141 --> 00:06:45.298
costs with the benchmark for this deal.” I
don’t need to re-enter the deal ID; the

00:06:45.333 --> 00:06:46.846
card already identifies it.

00:06:47.261 --> 00:06:53.961
Now ask: “Was it the storage rate or more
billable days?” We have not started a new

00:06:53.996 --> 00:06:59.111
conversation. The analyst still knows the
deal and the previous question. Check the

00:06:59.146 --> 00:07:03.864
answer against the cost breakdown rather
than taking the explanation on trust.

00:07:04.279 --> 00:07:10.503
Open a completed deal and ask, “Prepare a
Word report on this deal.” When the reply

00:07:10.538 --> 00:07:15.980
appears, scroll to Download Word. The file
contains the plan, the actual result and

00:07:16.015 --> 00:07:21.456
the biggest sources of variance for this
deal, at this snapshot. Here, the largest

00:07:21.491 --> 00:07:24.586
adverse cost variance is transport to the
port:

00:07:24.621 --> 00:07:27.715
about three point one million rubles above
plan.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:38.516
Next, go back to the payment calendar.
Choose Delay and measures and click

00:07:38.551 --> 00:07:43.519
Calculate scenario. Open Ask AI about
section from the calendar itself. That

00:07:43.554 --> 00:07:49.357
gives the analyst the current scenario and
filters. Ask: “When is the first cash

00:07:49.392 --> 00:07:52.276
shortfall in this scenario?” You don’t
have

00:07:52.311 --> 00:07:55.612
to enter the delay and recovery
assumptions again.

00:07:56.027 --> 00:08:01.027
The first gap is on the twenty-first of
November, at about twenty-three point one

00:08:01.062 --> 00:08:05.343
million rubles. The worst point comes
later, on the twenty-eighth of December:

00:08:05.378 --> 00:08:09.299
about three hundred and thirty-three point
four million rubles remain uncovered.

00:08:09.334 --> 00:08:13.615
There are forty-seven days with a
shortfall across the forecast. The first

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:19.370
date tells you when action is needed; the
worst point tells you how much funding you

00:08:19.405 --> 00:08:22.247
may have to find. Switch back to the

00:08:22.282 --> 00:08:25.484
calendar to check those figures before
making a decision.

00:08:33.899 --> 00:08:39.356
Now generate an Excel report for the
credit facility. Open it and check the

00:08:39.391 --> 00:08:44.455
snapshot, the selected deals and the
labelled sheets. Here you can see the

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.593
month-by-month balances and the cost of
debt. CSV

00:08:47.628 --> 00:08:51.123
is useful when you only need the
underlying rows.

00:08:56.538 --> 00:09:01.972
To send a report, choose the attachment
and check the email preview first. The

00:09:02.007 --> 00:09:07.440
preview shows the recipient and what will
be included. You can include the latest

00:09:07.475 --> 00:09:10.174
conversation in the message. We’ll leave
this

00:09:10.209 --> 00:09:12.908
demonstration at the preview and send
nothing.

00:09:13.307 --> 00:09:19.180
The values shown in the dashboard do not
come from a language model. The

00:09:19.215 --> 00:09:23.822
application starts with linked rows for
deals, lots, costs, milestones, payments

00:09:23.857 --> 00:09:28.886
and funding. The calculation uses shipped
weight, price, the quality discount and

00:09:28.921 --> 00:09:34.372
exchange rate for revenue. It deducts the
seller’s costs and interest for margin.

00:09:34.787 --> 00:09:40.569
The same calculation feeds the charts, the
report and the analyst’s tools. DeepSeek

00:09:40.604 --> 00:09:45.938
explains results and answers follow-up
questions. To verify an answer without the

00:09:45.973 --> 00:09:52.203
model, open the source tables or download
the snapshot. You can also download the

00:09:52.238 --> 00:09:53.993
synthetic benchmark rates separately.

00:09:57.408 --> 00:10:03.179
There is a plan for each deal. There is
not a separate approved company budget,

00:10:03.214 --> 00:10:08.598
planned cash balance or set of manager
targets. Those inputs would be needed for

00:10:08.633 --> 00:10:11.308
a full company-wide plan versus actual
analysis.

00:10:11.723 --> 00:10:17.427
To try the demo yourself, start with one
alert. Find the deal, open its cost lines

00:10:17.462 --> 00:10:22.447
and decide whether to check the rate, the
quantity or the schedule first. Then

00:10:22.482 --> 00:10:27.468
return to the cash forecast and shift
buyer receipts by twenty-one days. Look at

00:10:27.503 --> 00:10:32.131
when the gap starts and how much remains
uncovered after the response scenario.

00:10:32.546 --> 00:10:38.354
The link below opens the English demo. If
you want to try this with your own

00:10:38.389 --> 00:10:42.737
portfolio, start with one concrete
problem, such as storage costs or a

00:10:42.772 --> 00:10:45.659
payment gap. That makes it easier to
identify

00:10:45.694 --> 00:10:48.580
the records you need before integrating
the application.
